    "# Moore’s Law Linear Regression\n",
    "\n",
    "You are given a dataset describing Moore’s Law, stored in the file **`./datat/moore.csv`**.  \n",
    "Each record contains information about the **year**, **transistor count**, and other details for integrated circuits.  \n",
    "Your task is to implement a simple **linear regression model** in **PyTorch** to estimate the rate at which transistor counts have historically increased.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n",
    "---\n",
    "\n",
    "### Expected Output\n",
    "\n",
    "- Scatter plots of data before and after preprocessing  \n",
    "- A decreasing loss curve across epochs  \n",
    "- A final fitted regression line  \n",
    "- Calculated doubling‑time value (approximately **2 years** for real Moore’s Law data)\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "#### Your answer:"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [# Moore's Law Linear Regression in PyTorch

import torch
import torch.nn as nn
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import pandas as pd
import numpy as np

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Load and preprocess data
# --------------------------------------------------------------------

# Load data
df = pd.read_csv("./datat/moore.csv", sep="\t", header=None)

# Some datasets use tab or multiple spaces, clean it up
df.columns = ["Name", "TransistorCount", "Year", "Company", "Process", "Area"]

# Remove commas and non-numeric symbols in transistor count
df["TransistorCount"] = (
    df["TransistorCount"]
    .astype(str)
    .str.replace(",", "")
    .str.extract(r"(\d+)")
    .astype(float)
)

# Keep only numeric columns we need
X = df["Year"].values
Y = df["TransistorCount"].values

# Drop NaN
mask = ~np.isnan(Y)
X, Y = X[mask], Y[mask]

# Log-transform transistor count (Moore’s law is exponential)
Y_log = np.log(Y)

# Normalize years (helps training)
X_mean, X_std = X.mean(), X.std()
X_norm = (X - X_mean) / X_std

# Convert to PyTorch tensors
X_tensor = torch.tensor(X_norm, dtype=torch.float32).view(-1, 1)
Y_tensor = torch.tensor(Y_log, dtype=torch.float32).view(-1, 1)

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Visualize raw data
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
plt.scatter(X, Y, color="blue")
plt.title("Raw Transistor Counts over Years")
plt.xlabel("Year")
plt.ylabel("Transistor Count")
plt.yscale("log")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Define linear regression model
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
model = nn.Linear(1, 1)
criterion = nn.MSELoss()
optimizer = torch.optim.SGD(model.parameters(), lr=0.05)

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. Train model
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
losses = []
epochs = 2000

for epoch in range(epochs):
    # Forward pass
    Y_pred = model(X_tensor)
    loss = criterion(Y_pred, Y_tensor)
    losses.append(loss.item())

    # Backward pass
    optimizer.zero_grad()
    loss.backward()
    optimizer.step()

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. Plot loss curve
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
plt.figure(figsize=(7, 4))
plt.plot(losses)
plt.title("Training Loss Curve")
plt.xlabel("Epoch")
plt.ylabel("MSE Loss")
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. Plot fitted regression line
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
with torch.no_grad():
    Y_fit = model(X_tensor).numpy()

plt.figure(figsize=(8, 5))
plt.scatter(X, Y_log, label="Log(Transistor Count)", alpha=0.7)
plt.plot(X, Y_fit, color="red", label="Fitted Line")
plt.title("Linear Fit in Log-space (Moore's Law)")
plt.xlabel("Year")
plt.ylabel("log(Transistor Count)")
plt.legend()
plt.grid(True)
plt.show()

# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. Compute doubling time
# --------------------------------------------------------------------
# Model: log(N) = a + b * (Year - mean)/std
# So in real scale: N ∝ exp(b/std * Year)
b = model.weight.item() / X_std
doubling_time = np.log(2) / b

print(f"Estimated doubling time: {doubling_time:.2f} years")]
  }
